2
00:00:18.222 --> 00:00:22.614
Now that we've covered some of the basic
ideas that broadly defined stock options.

3
00:00:22.614 --> 00:00:27.116
Let's now address some specific terms and
meanings as well as examine the roles and

4
00:00:27.116 --> 00:00:30.280
obligations of buyers and
sellers of options contracts.

5
00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.788
A stock option is basically a contract
whereby a buyer and seller agree

6
00:00:36.788 --> 00:00:42.070
to a specified number of shares of
a particular stock at a fixed price.

7
00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.960
And over a defined period.

8
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:49.200
There are two main types of
option contracts, calls and puts.

9
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.480
We'll explore how these types of options
are used by traders in a later video.

10
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.148
But for now,
we can define a call contract,

11
00:00:55.148 --> 00:00:58.477
as one that gives the holder
of the contract the right.

12
00:00:58.477 --> 00:01:02.306
But not the obligation to buy
a stock at a predetermined price,

13
00:01:02.306 --> 00:01:04.810
over a given period of time.

14
00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:09.553
While a put option offers its holder the
right but again not the obligation to sell

15
00:01:09.553 --> 00:01:13.400
the stock at a predetermined price,
over a given period of time.

16
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:15.925
Stock options have buyers and

17
00:01:15.925 --> 00:01:18.943
sellers not unlike any other
other financial transaction.

18
00:01:18.943 --> 00:01:23.015
Each with their own responsibilities for
their side of the contract.

19
00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:27.123
For example, if you purchase an options
contract, you are then considered

20
00:01:27.123 --> 00:01:30.797
the holder or owner of that contract and
long in an option position.

21
00:01:30.797 --> 00:01:34.978
As the contract owner again you have
the right but not the obligation to buy or

22
00:01:34.978 --> 00:01:36.554
sell the underlying stock.

23
00:01:36.554 --> 00:01:41.139
Depending on the type of option that
you've bought, whether a call or a put.

24
00:01:41.139 --> 00:01:45.940
And as a buyer you'll pay what is
called a premium to the seller.

25
00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.902
We'll take a closer look at this premium
in our next video, but for now you should

26
00:01:49.902 --> 00:01:54.330
keep in mind that this payment is the same
as the market price for the contract.

27
00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.950
And this can fluctuate,
with certain changes in market dynamics.

28
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.019
Now as the contract owner,

29
00:02:01.019 --> 00:02:06.430
you have the choice to exercise your
option under the terms of your agreement.

30
00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:10.766
That is you can take delivery or
deliver, that certain number of

31
00:02:10.766 --> 00:02:15.790
underlying shares at or
before the time the contract expires.

32
00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.863
Or you can let the contract
expire at its expiration date,

33
00:02:18.863 --> 00:02:20.630
without exercising that right.

34
00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:25.449
The option holder also has the right
to sell the option to another buyer,

35
00:02:25.449 --> 00:02:30.200
during the lifetime of the contract,
or allow it to expire worthless.

36
00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:34.369
Now on the other side of
the contract is the seller,

37
00:02:34.369 --> 00:02:39.800
who may be referred to as the contract
writer, and short the option position.

38
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.967
As the contract writer, you are obligated
to fulfill the terms of your contract with

39
00:02:43.967 --> 00:02:47.880
its holder, should the buyer
decide to exercise their right.

40
00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.574
The premium you receive from the buyer
is essentially in exchange for

41
00:02:51.574 --> 00:02:52.600
this obligation.

42
00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.430
So what happens if a buyer
exercises their right.

43
00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:02.519
Well, depending on the type of contract
sold, you as an options contract writer.

44
00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:04.145
Would be required to either buy or

45
00:03:04.145 --> 00:03:07.930
sell the underlying shares at the
predetermined price that was agreed upon.

46
00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.350
Since there are a lot of moving parts on
options contract, let's map it all out.

47
00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:18.760
An options contract has two sides,
a buyer and a seller.

48
00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.041
In the case of a call,

49
00:03:20.041 --> 00:03:24.820
the owner has a right to purchase
stock at a predetermined fixed price.

50
00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:28.326
And for that right is exercised at or
before expiry,

51
00:03:28.326 --> 00:03:32.882
the seller is required to sell that
stock to the buyer at that price.

52
00:03:32.882 --> 00:03:37.310
Regardless of what that stocks price
is in the market at that time.

53
00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:43.760
For a put option, the buyer has a right to
sell stock at a predetermined fixed price.

54
00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.585
And if that right is exercised,

55
00:03:45.585 --> 00:03:49.650
the writer is obligated to buy
it under the same conditions.

56
00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:53.810
So what happens in the event
the option expires worthless.

57
00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.090
Well, if you're a buyer
of an options contract,

58
00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.590
you're going to pay that
premium to the writer.

59
00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.968
Or if the option expires worthless,

60
00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:04.200
that payment will end up
being your maximum loss.

61
00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:09.340
Now, if you're the seller receiving this
premium and an option expires worthless.

62
00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:12.919
This payment will be the most
you'll gain from the transaction.

63
00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:15.966
However since options don't
always expire worthless,

64
00:04:15.966 --> 00:04:20.420
there are several strategies a trader may
use to generate income or mitigate risk.

65
00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:24.060
Or face other potential positive or
adverse outcomes.

66
00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:27.900
And these will be discussed
further along in the course.

67
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.140
We'll also discuss how to
calculate your breakeven point.

68
00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:35.743
That is determining at what price
the underlying security in your options

69
00:04:35.743 --> 00:04:37.830
contract needs to be trading at.

70
00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:41.680
So that you don't lose or
make any gain from your investment.

71
00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.640
In the meantime in our next video,

72
00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.640
we'll show you how you can identify
options on a trading platform.

73
00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:53.670
And we'll also further define their
